  "summary": "The Tempe City Council voted 6-1 to permanently ban the city from using automated license plate reader technology, blocking law enforcement use, funding, and future contracts, and ordering destruction of existing ALPR data. Tempe is the first Arizona city to codify such a ban, following its earlier decision to end its Flock contract covering 33 cameras. ASU's 20 Flock cameras on its Tempe campus are unaffected.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The council approved a ban on the cameras in a 6-1 vote, with Vice Mayor Arlene Chin being the lone holdout.",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "The new ordinance blocks law enforcement use, funding and future contracts related to ALPR systems. The law destroys existing ALPR data and puts the ban into city code.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "We have an opportunity to be at the forefront of these bans of what I believe is a larger movement",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "The ban won't affect the 20 Flock cameras that Arizona State University has on its Tempe campus.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    }
  ],
